{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"agent_message","text":"{\n  \"A\": \"Own ID-resolution research. Use de3ce23ea89e4677 as the leading design and retain 93500ba1fd334b9b as the simpler comparator. Provide one graph-local ID-to-record-index interface usable by B and C. Compare bounded direct addressing, open addressing, and the small-graph linear fallback through the unchanged measurement system. Base dispatch only on actual input structure, not particular IDs or test cases. Preserve first-record duplicate resolution, missing-ID behavior, arbitrary signed IDs, overflow-safe range calculations, and allocation-failure fallback. Avoid additional lookup structures unless their complete construction and execution cost demonstrably helps.\",\n  \"B\": \"Own demanded-chain evaluation and suffix reuse. Develop the explicit-stack, completed-float cache from 32cf526d78ff47cf against A's lookup interface; retain ce564278bc344543 as an independently measured alternative. Resolve each demanded node's successor once, carry its record index directly, and share completed suffixes across queries without eagerly processing unreachable nodes. Compare against indexed recursion because cache initialization and stack storage can outweigh reuse. Unwind strictly as float parent_value plus completed_child; preserve terminal values without adding zero. Preserve neighbor order, strict comparisons, and selected-ID -1 termination. Keep cache state graph-local and avoid introducing shared-cache races.\",\n  \"C\": \"Own input and neighbor-decoding overhead. The existing bulk parser and parallel-query candidates did not improve baseline, so prioritize low-overhead serial experiments rather than more threading. Evaluate the serial bulk-reader/strtof portion of 643ebfd679794449 with indexed evaluation, where parsing may become more important. Separately investigate scanning immutable neighbor text on demand without strcpy or strtok_r, converting each token once and preserving the original delimiter and integer-conversion semantics. Retain libc float conversion, original query spelling, and record order. Handle single-node graphs and empty neighbor lists safely. Do not assume parallel parsing will amortize its setup cost; retain it only with measured integrated benefit.\",\n  \"G\": \"Integrate selectively, using de3ce23ea89e4677 at 5.648 ms as the incumbent rather than assuming every tree contributes a gain. First measure A plus B's demanded-chain cache, replacing B's packed linear-search array with A's shared index. Separately measure A plus C's serial parsing changes. Test the combined version only as a distinct candidate: constituent improvements are not additive. Preserve the standalone A and B alternatives and choose the fastest correct measured program, including an unchanged constituent if integration loses. Do not graft parallel queries onto an unsynchronized shared cache; on four cores, parallelism must justify both coordination and startup costs. Validate through the unchanged oracle and tests, preserving original query text/order and %f output. Keep compiler, target, permissions, budgets, and harness unchanged; use only general input-dependent algorithms.\",\n  \"analysis\": \"The relevant task is Problem F, not the other contest problems. The source follows the first neighbor with the greatest strictly positive immediate float value; it does not maximize total downstream cost. Missing IDs contribute zero, duplicate IDs resolve to the first record, and a selected ID of -1 terminates traversal. Recursive float grouping and terminal signed zero must remain intact. Baseline is 11.571 ms; hybrid indexing reaches 5.648 ms, hash-only indexing 5.742 ms, and packed ARM64 NEON lookup with caching 5.885 ms. Caching alone reaches 10.530 ms; bulk/parallel parsing and parallel queries reach 11.601 and 11.777 ms. Thus lookup elimination has the strongest evidence, while caching and parsing remain integration hypotheses. The close leading means do not establish a robust ranking without comparable repeat evidence. No integration measurements are supplied, and four baseline correctness cases do not establish correctness for every valid input.\"\n}"}}
